+  "id": "ISS-003",
+  "impact": "`createApp` returns only `{ handle }` (src/http/handler.ts:29-34). When no queue is passed it builds its own (`queue: JobQueue = new JobQueue()`, line 17) and keeps it private, and nothing anywhere in the repo ever calls `JobQueue.run`. So a default-constructed app accepts POST /reports, enqueues a job, returns 202 with a jobId, and that job is never executed and never will be -- the queue grows unbounded and the 202 is a promise the service cannot keep. A caller can only work around it by constructing the queue itself and passing it in. Found during T-2 code review: the T-2 plan proposed threading the logging sink into that default queue, and the change turned out to be unpinnable by any test precisely because the queue is unreachable, so the change was reverted rather than shipped untested. Pre-existing and out of T-2's scope. Likely fix: return the queue (or a `runJobs()` handle) from createApp, with a test that a job enqueued through POST /reports actually runs; that would also make the sink-threading testable.",
